Subject: [PATCH] input: wait for udev initialization in startup discovery

Startup discovery added every hidraw and evdev device the udev enumerator
returned at once. A node can exist before udevd finishes its rules, e.g.
when a USB device re-enumerates while the daemon starts. Such a device has
only its kernel uevent properties, and UdevDevice caches that snapshot, so
configs that match udev properties (ID_INPUT_JOYSTICK, ID_PATH) never match
it. No later event corrects it: the devnode watcher only sees creates.

Add initialized devices at once as before. Re-probe the rest by syspath
until udev initializes them, then add them once with fresh properties. Drop
a device that is removed while waiting. Bound the wait: past the deadline a
device is added as is, which is what upstream did immediately.

Observed on a Galaxy Tab S6: a Razer Kishi V2 re-enumerated 0.5 s before
InputPlumber started; its udev database was written 1.9 s after the scan,
the cached properties held 5 keys and no composite was created.
Regression coverage uses real USB gadget, delayed udev rules and DBus in
Korri's inputplumber-udev-startup VM test.

diff --git a/src/input/manager.rs b/src/input/manager.rs
index 2e847d7..3fefc17 100644
--- a/src/input/manager.rs
+++ b/src/input/manager.rs
@@ -59,6 +59,8 @@ use crate::watcher::WatchEvent;
